WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.334 --> 00:00:02.802
-Our first guest tonight
is an Associate Justice

00:00:02.802 --> 00:00:04.236
to the United States
Supreme Court.

00:00:04.236 --> 00:00:07.140
Her new children's book,
"Just Help: How to Build

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:08.641
a Better World"
was published today.

00:00:08.641 --> 00:00:11.678
Please welcome back to the show
justice Sonia Sotomayor.

00:00:11.678 --> 00:00:15.081
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:15.081 --> 00:00:17.483
Hello, Justice!
How are you?

00:00:17.483 --> 00:00:19.652
-I'm well, Seth.
How are you doing?

00:00:19.652 --> 00:00:21.253
-I'm doing good.
Are you -- are you

00:00:21.253 --> 00:00:24.023
technically in chambers
right now?

00:00:24.023 --> 00:00:26.125
-I'm technically
in the courthouse,

00:00:26.125 --> 00:00:28.361
not in my private office.

00:00:28.361 --> 00:00:30.630
-Okay, because I would love --
I would love for you

00:00:30.630 --> 00:00:33.132
to say right now that I'm seeing
you in your chambers

00:00:33.132 --> 00:00:34.734
because that is like --

00:00:34.734 --> 00:00:37.837
The dream in my life is to be an
attorney and have a judge say,

00:00:37.837 --> 00:00:39.539
"See me in my chambers."

00:00:39.539 --> 00:00:40.973
-[ Laughs ]

00:00:40.973 --> 00:00:42.342
-If you want,
I'll tell you that,

00:00:42.342 --> 00:00:44.110
and you can come visit me
at some point.

00:00:44.110 --> 00:00:45.612
-One day I would love to be.

00:00:45.612 --> 00:00:48.448
I'd love to be in someone's
chambers, just once.

00:00:48.448 --> 00:00:49.682
-Just once, come visit.

00:00:49.682 --> 00:00:51.250
[ Laughs ]

00:00:51.250 --> 00:00:53.319
Congratulations on the book,
"Just Help."

00:00:53.319 --> 00:00:55.454
You had a previous book,
"Just Ask."

00:00:55.454 --> 00:00:57.656
I see a theme here.

00:00:57.656 --> 00:00:59.325
What was the difference with
"Just Help?"

00:00:59.325 --> 00:01:00.727
And did you always know
that you were going

00:01:00.727 --> 00:01:02.929
to write a second book?

00:01:02.929 --> 00:01:04.164
-A third.

00:01:04.164 --> 00:01:06.966
I started -- this is my third
children's book.

00:01:06.966 --> 00:01:10.537
The first was a story
of my life, "Turning Pages"

00:01:10.537 --> 00:01:12.338
and how books influenced me.

00:01:12.338 --> 00:01:16.108
As I think you might remember,
"Just Ask" was a book that I had

00:01:16.108 --> 00:01:18.377
in my head for 30 years.

00:01:18.377 --> 00:01:22.148
And when it came to life,
it was satisfying that dream.

00:01:22.148 --> 00:01:25.818
And this one just helped,
really, as a continuation

00:01:25.818 --> 00:01:30.890
of my life commitment
as a justice to inspiring kids

00:01:30.890 --> 00:01:33.893
to know their power
to change the world,

00:01:33.893 --> 00:01:36.995
and I think that's the most
important work I do.

00:01:36.995 --> 00:01:40.833
Yeah, the opinion writing helps
a little bit in my work.

00:01:40.833 --> 00:01:46.005
But I do think that writing for
kids and writing about topics

00:01:46.005 --> 00:01:48.841
that I think that can inspire
them to believe in

00:01:48.841 --> 00:01:51.511
themselves, that that's the most
important thing

00:01:51.511 --> 00:01:53.546
I can do outside
the courtroom.

00:01:53.546 --> 00:01:55.748
-When it comes to being...
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:55.748 --> 00:01:57.517
Absolutely.

00:01:57.517 --> 00:02:01.487
When it comes to being helpful,
I heard that is something

00:02:01.487 --> 00:02:05.191
that your mother instilled
in you at a young age.

00:02:05.191 --> 00:02:07.426
Did you appreciate as a kid
how important it was

00:02:07.426 --> 00:02:09.595
to be helpful
to those around you?

00:02:09.595 --> 00:02:12.498
-No, because you learn
by example,

00:02:12.498 --> 00:02:14.333
and I saw my mother doing it.

00:02:14.333 --> 00:02:18.070
I saw my grandmother and my Aunt
doing it in different ways.

00:02:18.070 --> 00:02:23.976
And watching others who I love
taking small acts of improving

00:02:23.976 --> 00:02:28.380
the world, I never understood
that one person

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:33.352
doesn't change the world, but
everybody working together can.

00:02:33.352 --> 00:02:37.089
And that's what my mother taught
me, by her example of being

00:02:37.089 --> 00:02:41.361
the nurse to the entire
neighborhoods we lived in,

00:02:41.361 --> 00:02:44.263
to our family,
but to friends and neighbors

00:02:44.263 --> 00:02:45.764
and neighbors she didn't know.

00:02:45.764 --> 00:02:48.500
People would hear about my mom
and that she was a nurse.

00:02:48.500 --> 00:02:51.270
They'd walk up to our door and
knock, and I'd answer it.

00:02:51.270 --> 00:02:52.872
I didn't know
who the person was,

00:02:52.872 --> 00:02:55.842
neither did mom,
and they'd say something like,

00:02:55.842 --> 00:02:57.609
"I'm having a medical issue.

00:02:57.609 --> 00:02:59.578
Could I talk to you?"

00:02:59.578 --> 00:03:01.981
And inevitably, it would be
they needed some help

00:03:01.981 --> 00:03:04.850
taking medication, and my mother
would always say yes

00:03:04.850 --> 00:03:07.920
and just do it out of the
goodness of her heart.

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:12.025
I had an aunt, my mother's
sister, who was very, very poor.

00:03:12.025 --> 00:03:17.496
She lived in the South Bronx,
and her income was decidedly

00:03:17.496 --> 00:03:20.733
lesser than almost any other
member of the family.

00:03:20.733 --> 00:03:23.902
And yet I watched her every
Thanksgiving and Christmas

00:03:23.902 --> 00:03:26.972
going through her kitchen
and my mother's kitchen

00:03:26.972 --> 00:03:29.775
and putting together packages
of food to give people

00:03:29.775 --> 00:03:31.877
less fortunate than us.

00:03:31.877 --> 00:03:34.847
And I didn't see those acts
of what people think of

00:03:34.847 --> 00:03:39.752
as kindness, but they're really
civic participation.

00:03:39.752 --> 00:03:42.554
They're participating in making
your neighborhood

00:03:42.554 --> 00:03:44.356
and your community better.

00:03:44.356 --> 00:03:47.894
And so as I grew up, I figured
out, you know something?

00:03:47.894 --> 00:03:49.528
They set an example for me.

00:03:49.528 --> 00:03:52.131
Now I have to follow it.

00:03:52.131 --> 00:03:54.433
And you can do small things,
like mom.

00:03:54.433 --> 00:03:56.402
They weren't so small,
but they were very important

00:03:56.402 --> 00:03:58.704
to the people
they were doing it for.

00:03:58.704 --> 00:04:03.042
But you could take small steps,
then bigger steps.

00:04:03.042 --> 00:04:05.144
And this book, "Just Help,"
is about some of those

00:04:05.144 --> 00:04:07.580
bigger steps kids can take.

00:04:07.580 --> 00:04:09.381
just because you're a kid
doesn't mean

00:04:09.381 --> 00:04:11.517
you're not powerful.

00:04:11.517 --> 00:04:15.154
[ Applause ]
-Now, I'm wondering,

00:04:15.154 --> 00:04:18.190
have you heard from kids who
read your books?

00:04:18.190 --> 00:04:20.926
Are they aware they are reading
a children's book

00:04:20.926 --> 00:04:23.228
that is written
by a Supreme Court justice?

00:04:23.228 --> 00:04:26.298
Or do they just think you're a
very successful children's

00:04:26.298 --> 00:04:28.367
author who also has a side gig?

00:04:28.367 --> 00:04:31.403
[ Laughter ]

00:04:31.403 --> 00:04:33.572
-I don't know what the kids
initially think,

00:04:33.572 --> 00:04:38.144
but I do know their parents
kind of tell them along the way.

00:04:38.144 --> 00:04:41.447
So by the time
they finish the book,

00:04:41.447 --> 00:04:44.650
they know what my day job is.

00:04:44.650 --> 00:04:50.055
But I did have a very young
child whose mother told me

00:04:50.055 --> 00:04:54.793
her favorite book was about me,
and her mother read it to her

00:04:54.793 --> 00:04:56.729
every single night.

00:04:56.729 --> 00:04:59.398
And she asked me whether
she could bring her child

00:04:59.398 --> 00:05:02.201
to meet me,
and I said absolutely yes.

00:05:02.201 --> 00:05:07.440
And the little girl walked
into my office and looked at me,

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:12.445
looked all around, and couldn't
find the figure of me

00:05:12.445 --> 00:05:14.313
that she had seen in the book.

00:05:14.313 --> 00:05:16.249
She didn't realize
I had grown up.

00:05:16.249 --> 00:05:17.316
[ Laughter ]

00:05:17.316 --> 00:05:18.918
So she was a little
disappointed.

00:05:18.918 --> 00:05:20.953
I've received letters
from her since,

00:05:20.953 --> 00:05:22.155
telling me about her life.

00:05:22.155 --> 00:05:24.056
I think she figured it out.

00:05:24.056 --> 00:05:28.127
But you know, it's hard
to know what kids think.

00:05:28.127 --> 00:05:30.062
They see you on television.

00:05:30.062 --> 00:05:31.697
And sometimes they wonder,

00:05:31.697 --> 00:05:36.268
how is it that you come out of
the television to talk to them?

00:05:36.268 --> 00:05:39.739
-I find with my kids, the moment
I'm having now is, you know,

00:05:39.739 --> 00:05:42.074
a lot of actors who are voices
in animated movies

00:05:42.074 --> 00:05:44.242
that my kids watch
have been on this show.

00:05:44.242 --> 00:05:46.745
And I'll say, "You know, daddy
talked to that actor,"

00:05:46.745 --> 00:05:48.147
and I'll show a clip.

00:05:48.147 --> 00:05:50.183
And they just immediately are
like, "I don't like this."

00:05:50.183 --> 00:05:52.451
[ Laughter ]

00:05:52.451 --> 00:05:56.622
I don't like seeing that bear
as a human being.

00:05:56.622 --> 00:05:59.525
-I expect that that is true,

00:05:59.525 --> 00:06:03.696
and I know kids must have that
reaction every once in a while.

00:06:03.696 --> 00:06:06.064
I also tell kids all the time,
"You know, I'm going to do

00:06:06.064 --> 00:06:07.600
something that disappoints you.

00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:10.536
I'm going to issue --" and I
tell adults this, too.

00:06:10.536 --> 00:06:13.272
"I'm going to issue a decision
someday that you don't like.

00:06:13.272 --> 00:06:16.476
That's the nature of my job,
to do what the law says.

00:06:16.476 --> 00:06:20.179
That doesn't always accord to
what you may think is right.

00:06:20.179 --> 00:06:22.982
So you have to understand that
I'm a human being."

00:06:22.982 --> 00:06:28.687
And I think when you step out
of the TV and present yourself

00:06:28.687 --> 00:06:33.058
as a person, that kids might
have a little bit of a hard time

00:06:33.058 --> 00:06:36.295
accepting that you're
a real human being

00:06:36.295 --> 00:06:39.965
and not just an image
on that screen.

00:06:39.965 --> 00:06:41.900
But your kids know
the real human being,

00:06:41.900 --> 00:06:45.304
and I'm sure they prefer that
to this personality.

00:06:45.304 --> 00:06:46.171
What do you think?

00:06:46.171 --> 00:06:47.873
-I think so, too.

00:06:47.873 --> 00:06:49.842
You know, I want to say
something.

00:06:49.842 --> 00:06:52.778
You are a real human being that
I always enjoy talking to,

00:06:52.778 --> 00:06:55.848
despite the fact
that you're a Yankees fan.

00:06:55.848 --> 00:06:59.018
And that's very hard
for me to say.

00:06:59.018 --> 00:07:00.186
-Seth.
-Yes.

00:07:00.186 --> 00:07:03.289
Before you razz me
for last year,

00:07:03.289 --> 00:07:06.725
remember one very
important thing.

00:07:06.725 --> 00:07:10.929
The arc of the universe always
bends towards justice,

00:07:10.929 --> 00:07:12.798
and we will come back.

00:07:12.798 --> 00:07:15.301
[ Laughter ]

00:07:15.301 --> 00:07:16.502
-Then I'll leave it at that.

00:07:16.502 --> 00:07:18.236
I will leave it at that.

00:07:18.236 --> 00:07:20.972
Justice Sonia Sotomayor,
everybody.

00:07:20.972 --> 00:07:24.176
"Just Help: How to Build a
Better World" is available

00:07:24.176 --> 00:07:26.512
wherever you buy your books,
but remember to support

00:07:26.512 --> 00:07:27.747
local and independent
book stores.

